<center>John Fitzgerald Kennedy</center>
<p>
John Fitzgerald Kennedy was born in Brookline, Massachusetts, on May 29,
 1917. Kennedy’s family had emigrated from Ireland in 1849 and his
grandfathers were important political figures in Boston.
Kennedy's father was a highly successful businessman who later served
as ambassador to Great Britain.
</p><p>
Kennedy graduated from Harvard with a science degree in 1940.
In 1941 he joined the Navy and was sent to the South Pacific when
the USA entered the 2nd World War.
He was given the commandment of a torpedo boat which was hit by
 a Japanese destroyer. Two of his crew were killed, but Kennedy and
the other 6 members of the crew managed to swim to an island several
 miles from where the incident had occurred.
Since he had been badly injured, he was then sent back to the USA
and continued to work as an instructor in the Navy.
</p><p>
Member of the Democratic party, in 1946 he was elected to the
House of Representatives and in 1952 to the Senate. Kennedy was a
strong advocate of civil rights and social welfare legislation
in the Senate.
</p><p>
The following year he married Jacqueline Bouvier who bore him 4
children in the following years. However only two survived to
infancy: Caroline and John.
In 1960 he became the presidential candidate for the Democratic party.
 In 1961 He was to become the first Roman Catholic president who
had ever been elected and the second youngest president of the United States of America.
Kennedy’s presidency was marked by the Cold War. The Soviet Union
had deployed long range missiles in Cuba which led Kennedy to impose
a naval blockade on Cuba to stop Soviet ships from carrying weapons
to Cuba and also instructed the Air Force to prepare for attacks on
Cuba and the Soviet Union. During that time the world waited
anxiously for nuclear war to break out, however the Soviet president,
 Khrushchev, ordered his ships to turn back before they reached the
US Navy. This confrontation would mark a change in the development
of the Cold War as it brought about a series of negotiations to
 reduce nuclear missiles between the Soviet leaders and the American
 presidents.
Another burning issue during Kennedy’s presidency was the war in
Vietnam. Despite the deployment of 12 000 troops by the end of 1962
 and other measures, the Vietnamese National Liberation Front
continued to gain ground among the peasants and the USA would be
 bogged down in that war for another 10 years.
</p><p>
Welfare reform with the Medicaid program also constituted a major
part of Kennedy’s program, however this bill did not get through.
Kennedy’s civil rights bill was brought to congress in 1963.
In a speech on television on 11th June, Kennedy pointed out that:
</p><p>
"The Negro baby born in America today, regardless of the section of
the nation in which he is born, has about one-half as much chance of
completing high school as a white baby born in the same place on the
same day; one third as much chance of completing college; one third
as much chance of becoming a professional man; twice as much chance
of becoming unemployed; about one-seventh as much chance of earning
$10,000 a year; a life expectancy which is seven years shorter;
and the prospects of earning only half as much."
</p><p>
He had barely had time to work on the civil rights bill as he was
assassinated in Dallas on 22nd November, 1963 - it had been decided
 that Kennedy and his party, including his wife and
Vice President Lyndon B. Johnson, would travel in a procession of
cars through the business district of Dallas. Kennedy was killed
while riding in an open car. His putative assassin
(a man called H. Lee Oswald) was shot while he was being transferred
to prison two days after Kennedy had been killed. Who shot Kennedy
and who was responsible for the assassination of Oswald remains a
mystery.
</p>
